WEBVTT

00:00:00.349 --> 00:00:30.979
<v Speaker 1>15 rounds of Swiss play have wrapped up here at Pro Tour London 2005 and after the dust settled we're left with eight men standing. Unusually, top 8 was a clean break. All 8 top 8 competitors at 34 points are better, so no one was left out in the cold on the back of a random pair down or fickle tie Breakers. This is the top eight you had to earn your way into finishing in first position as Masashi Aweso, a name we're almost starting to take for granted as a Sunday player. This marks Reasor's 5th Pro Tour top eight, with the most recent coming in Pro Tour Columbus 2005. Oliso is considered by many to be 1 of, if not the best</v>

00:00:31.049 --> 00:00:35.159
<v Speaker 1>player in the world and no one will be happy to sit down across from him the quarters tomorrow.</v>

00:00:35.789 --> 00:00:46.389
<v Speaker 1>In 2nd place is another PT Columbus alum, Belgian Jeffrey Saran, who's a green blue Madness deck fell against eventual champion Pierre Canali there. Surance put up a stellar day too to get another shot at PT Glory.</v>

00:00:47.089 --> 00:01:10.819
<v Speaker 1>In 3rd place is the unquestioned crowd favorite two time Pro Tour Top 2 veteran Tommy Wallabies currently touring the UK as a stand up comic. Despite a layoff of more than a year, Tommy won his round 15 match to make the top 8. An exhilarating style, avoiding a loss by decking by hindering his own consuming vortex atop his library during the upkeep of the turn he otherwise would have drawn out. Tommy may not have played much recently, but he's looked very sharp all weekend.</v>

00:01:11.619 --> 00:01:13.909
<v Speaker 1>In fourth place is Czech Arno Zajac.</v>

00:01:14.559 --> 00:01:31.239
<v Speaker 1>Arnest blazed out of the gates with a perfect ten O record to start the tournament and Wallace's first PT top 8. He's no stranger to intense competition, having finished 44th in Nagoya and 6th in Grand Prix Paris 2004 out of almost 1600 players. Look for Zedeck to force Blue in the top 8 as he's willing to play it with any other color.</v>

00:01:32.019 --> 00:01:47.959
<v Speaker 1>In 5th place is Swede David Larson, claiming his first Pro level Top 8 qualifying the hard way via the PTQ circuit, Larson posted A6012 record on Day 2 to coast into Sunday. Larson may not have the Pro Tour experience, but the way he was dispatching household names today to take him lightly would be a mistake.</v>

00:01:48.759 --> 00:02:19.099
<v Speaker 1>In six positions, GP powerhouse Joshi Fajita making only his second Pro Tour top aide to go with a dozen of the Grand Prix variety. Kyoshi made the most of his star-studded playtest group in Japan and it's Pro Player Club invitation to rack up an impressive 11 three in one record, largely on the back of blue white Flyers. In 7th place, his longtime first timer Johann Sarjapur out of Omen, Sweden. Who else is from Omen? The guy who's been predicting Sarjapur for a top 8 for the last three Pro Tours? Fellow sweet Anton Johnson, John's open minded drafter with a lot of Pro Tour</v>

00:02:19.169 --> 00:02:43.829
<v Speaker 1>experience and Anton thinks he's that good. His chances on Sunday must be pretty solid. Claiming the last slot is another first timer Finn anti Mullin riding his top 32 from Pro Tour Philadelphia anti drafted wherever the best first pick led him to his day three birth. Melon considers his top eight chances to be quote quite even UN quote, but Tommy Wallabies identified Malin as the player who would least like to face in the top aide. Again, a powerful endorsement from a great player.</v>

00:02:45.289 --> 00:03:14.609
<v Speaker 1>A few other fan favorites fell just short of the mark. Eugene Harvey finished in the heartbreaking 9th spot and Bram Step bangers just behind him. Tighten lost in the final round of finished 15th and Richie Holland's uncooperative lands pushed him down to 20th position. Local Sam Gomersall squeezed into the six Pro Tour point ranges 31st place. Also of note, the Japanese put up another fantastic performance, placing 7 players in the top 32. So who's your pick for the top eight? Let us know in the forums and be sure to tune in tomorrow for our live webcast of the top 8 with pregame kicking off at 5:00,</v>

